WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:07.550
Within the first few days of Pokémon Go's
July 2016 launch, the app has already been

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:12.780
downloaded an estimated 7.5 million times
in the United States alone--and by the time

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:18.099
you're watching this, that number has skyrocketed.
Nintendo's stock soared as the game outperformed

00:00:18.099 --> 00:00:22.230
all expectations. People around the world
have gone crazy for the augmented-reality

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:26.720
installment in the long-running series, which
allows users to catch Pokémon, battle at

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.440
Gyms, and collect items in the world around
them. But Pokémon Go isn't all fun and games.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.129
Some players have found injuries, criminals,
and even death on their adventures.

00:00:36.129 --> 00:00:37.010
Gotta catch 'em all

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.510
For Android users, be wary when downloading
Pokémon Go from any source other than the

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:46.440
Google Play store. The Android operating system
allows users, particularly those who have

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.639
"rooted" devices, to download program files
from outside of the official app store and

00:00:50.639 --> 00:00:54.879
install them on their phones. For Pokémon
fans living in countries that can't download

00:00:54.879 --> 00:00:59.170
the app officially yet, these files are the
only way for them to get early access to the

00:00:59.170 --> 00:00:59.909
game.

00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:03.869
Additionally, some of these files are targeted
at players who want to game the system. These

00:01:03.869 --> 00:01:08.570
"cheat" versions of the app supposedly allow
them to spoof their GPS location in order

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:14.120
to visit Gyms and catch Pokémon without leaving
their couches. The bad news? Enterprising

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.140
hackers have taken advantage of the demand
for these files, releasing versions of Pokémon

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:22.120
Go packed with viruses that take control of
the user's phone.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:23.110
Home Invasion

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:29.310
Pokémon Go developers Niantic are also responsible
for another augmented-reality game, Ingress.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.720
In that app, users are encouraged to visit,
claim, and add local points of interest for

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.140
their team, like a global game of capture
the flag. These points of interest were re-used

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.740
by Niantic when developing the map used by
Pokémon Go. Unfortunately, this has led to

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:47.390
some mishaps and confusion for players trying
to visit certain Gyms and Pokéstops.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.990
In Australia, one local police station took
to social media to ask players to please stop

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.690
wandering into their station in search of
Pikachu. Massachusetts resident Boon Sheridan

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:01.680
has it even worse. He bought an old church
in 2015 and converted it into a home. The

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.810
church was previously an Ingress portal, and
is now a Gym in Pokémon Go. Sheridan reported

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:10.229
that he's lost count of the number of players
who have shown up to his residence at all

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:14.209
hours in the day and night. Sheridan has made
the best of the situation, venturing outside

00:02:14.209 --> 00:02:15.469
to meet some of the visitors.

00:02:15.469 --> 00:02:20.409
The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum
in Washington DC has taken a different stance

00:02:20.409 --> 00:02:25.370
on dedicated Pokemon hunters. While it probably
should go without saying, the Museum recently

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.939
issued a statement after an influx of misguided
visitors, saying:

00:02:28.939 --> 00:02:33.249
“We feel playing ‘Pokémon Go’ in a
memorial dedicated to the victims of Nazism

00:02:33.249 --> 00:02:37.370
is inappropriate. We encourage visitors to
use their phones to share and engage with

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.590
museum content while here. Technology can
be an important learning tool, but this game

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:46.180
falls outside of our educational and memorial
mission. We are looking into how the museum

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:47.849
can be removed from it.”

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:49.670
Don’t Be Tentacruel

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:54.219
Enterprising criminals in O'Fallon, Missouri
have used the allure of Pokémon Go to draw

00:02:54.219 --> 00:02:59.359
unsuspecting players into traps. These criminals
placed "lure modules" on Pokéstops in remote

00:02:59.359 --> 00:03:03.799
locations and waited for adventurers to come
running. The modules increase the chance of

00:03:03.799 --> 00:03:08.540
Pokémon appearing nearby, which in turn attracts
more trainers to the location to catch them.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:13.019
After an unlucky player showed up, the thieves
relieved them of their belongings at gunpoint.

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:17.099
"You fools! This wouldn't have happened if
you'd handed over your Pokemon nicely, but

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:22.919
now it's too late for that. So I'm going to
take those Pokemon and destroy you!"

00:03:22.919 --> 00:03:27.010
There were 11 such incidents in the O'Fallon
and St. Louis area over the span of a few

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:31.290
days. Eventually the criminals were captured
following another stick-up, and they were

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:35.489
charged with first-degree armed robbery and
Grand Theft Squirtle.

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:37.909
That's definitely not a water-type…

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:42.419
In rural Wyoming, 19-year old Pokémon Go
trainer Shayla Wiggins set out to catch some

00:03:42.419 --> 00:03:44.450
rare Pokemon near the Big Wind River.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:50.269
"I woke up this morning, and I wanted to go
get a water Pokemon, so I just got up and

00:03:50.269 --> 00:03:53.870
went for my little walk, for a walk to go
catch Pokemon."

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:57.809
When Wiggins realized she was standing just
a few steps away from a corpse, she called

00:03:57.809 --> 00:04:02.629
the police. No word on whether or not Wiggins
ever found that water-type Pokémon.

00:04:02.629 --> 00:04:04.199
Prepare for trouble

00:04:04.199 --> 00:04:07.669
Niantic may have foreseen some of the issues
related to would-be Pokémon trainers with

00:04:07.669 --> 00:04:11.599
their faces buried in their phones, especially
after the multiple tragic deaths associated

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:15.219
with Ingress. When you start up the game,
a warning cautions trainers to stay aware

00:04:15.219 --> 00:04:18.970
of their surroundings. But there have still
been reports of players who were a just little

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:23.660
too focused on catching that Flareon and ended
up with various injuries to show for it.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:27.690
Lindsay Plunkett, a 23-year-old waitress in
Asheville, North Carolina, tripped over a

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:32.410
cinderblock while playing the game and earned
a colorful bruise on her right shin. One Reddit

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:37.020
user claimed that their Pokémon Go adventure ended up with a trip to the local emergency room.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.360
"Oh no! Staryu looks like it's in real pain!"

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.060
"It doesn't even have a face, how can you
tell?"

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:47.150
And this is just a few days in. How long until
the Poke-death toll spikes?

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:49.520
Blasting off at the speed of light

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:53.370
While we expected people to get fired from
their jobs for playing too much Pokémon Go,

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:57.760
we weren't expecting someone to get fired
for NOT playing it. An Australian expatriate

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:03.190
had recently relocated to Singapore in order
to take a marketing job for 99.co, an online

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:06.970
real estate portal. When he found out that
Pokémon Go wasn’t available to play in

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:10.500
Singapore yet, he took to social media to
vent his frustration publicly.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:15.230
Naturally, many native Singaporeans took issue
with his language insulting their homeland,

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:19.810
and started arguing with him online. Instead
of apologizing for his outburst, he doubled

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:24.540
down, claiming that he was there for work
because the locals "can't even read" and were

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:27.040
only good at "mouthing off."

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:27.730
"Dah?"

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:29.130
"Oh no!"

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:33.120
It didn't take long for the man's employer
to find out about his offensive online behavior.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.270
99.co apologized, and he found himself fired
from his position the very same day, which

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:42.650
leaves him plenty of time to catch ‘em all.
Job applications, that is.

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:45.930
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:49.460
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know what crazy stories you’ve heard

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:50.520
about Pokemon Go...

